{
  "einkommen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "high",
        "detail": "\"earn\" is the action of verdienen and would license the wrong German: einkommen is not a transitive verb of earning, and *\"Ich komme 3000 Euro ein\" is not German. The verb is intransitive and formal — money, donations, or entries come in (\"die eingekommenen Spenden\"), or one petitions for something (\"um etwas einkommen\"). The money-arriving sense is not in candidate_glosses; index 1 is the unrelated sports sense of placing in a ranking.",
        "fix_gloss": "come in (of money)"
      }
    ]
  },
  "einkehren": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "\"sojourn\" is archaic English and loses what the verb actually names: stopping off at an inn or restaurant during a walk or journey (\"wir sind im Gasthaus eingekehrt\"). A learner reading \"sojourn\" would take it for a general word for staying somewhere, which is einkehren's figurative extension at best (\"Ruhe kehrte ein\"). Neither candidate gloss is usable; the correct sense is absent from the list.",
        "fix_gloss": "stop at an inn"
      }
    ]
  },
  "einreisen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "\"travel\" belongs to reisen, not einreisen, and bare \"enter\" collides with betreten/eintreten for entering a room. einreisen means specifically to cross a border into a country (\"nach Deutschland einreisen\"); the country is not an optional nuance but the whole content of the ein-. The single candidate gloss carries the same defect, so the fix is not an index swap.",
        "fix_gloss": "enter a country"
      }
    ]
  },
  "einschreiben": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "The literal \"write into, inscribe\" is kaikki's first-listed sense but the reading a learner meets is reflexive: sich einschreiben = to enroll or register, above all at a university. That sense is candidate_glosses index 2 (\"to enroll, enlist\"). The registered-mail sense at index 1 is the other one in daily use; the inscribing sense is the rarest of the three.",
        "fix_gloss": "enroll, register"
      }
    ]
  },
  "einschulen": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "medium",
        "detail": "The shipped gloss is the Austrian workplace sense (einschulen = einarbeiten, train someone for a job), which in Germany is rare. The standard meaning is enrolling a child in school — \"das Kind wird im September eingeschult\" — which is candidate_glosses index 1 (\"to accept a pupil into the first grade\"). The shipped phrasing is also a dictionary paraphrase rather than a gloss.",
        "fix_gloss": "enroll in school"
      }
    ]
  },
  "einschneiden": {
    "findings": [
      {
        "type": "bad_gloss",
        "severity": "low",
        "detail": "\"engrave\" points at gravieren and away from the core sense, which is simply cutting into a surface or making an incision (\"eine Kerbe einschneiden\"), plus the everyday physical sense of a strap or waistband digging in (\"der Riemen schneidet ein\"). \"carve\" is defensible for \"seinen Namen in die Rinde einschneiden\" and worth keeping alongside, but leading with the engraving reading misdirects.",
        "fix_gloss": "cut into, carve"
      }
    ]
  }
}

